WHAT: make Qt6::PrintSupport optional in KXmlGui (guarding the one file that
      uses it), and skip the KSandbox packaging-format lookup on WASI.
WHY:  two blockers, and the first is the reason KXmlGui was the highest-risk
      framework in this port.
      1. CMakeLists.txt:46 `find_package(Qt6 ... REQUIRED Widgets Xml Network
         PrintSupport)`. Unlike Svg -- a separate repo that a port-local
         sysroot overlay can supply, which is how qt-torrentfileeditor103 gets
         Core5Compat -- PrintSupport lives INSIDE qtbase. So the overlay rule
         does not save you: a port cannot add it without rebuilding the shared
         qtbase that every other Qt node in wk depends on. wk qtbase is
         FEATURE_printsupport=OFF because a wk node paints into one RGBA8
         surface and has no printer, no CUPS and no PDF spooler behind it.
         Exactly ONE file uses it: kshortcutseditor.cpp printShortcuts(), the
         "print this shortcut list" button, whose entire body already sits
         inside an `#ifndef _WIN32_WCE` because "one can t print on wince".
         The guard is widened to include HAVE_PRINTSUPPORT and the function
         becomes a no-op on this platform. Everything else in KShortcutsEditor
         -- editing, conflict detection, shortcut schemes -- is untouched.
         NOTE that Qt6::Network stays REQUIRED and resolves: plugins/qt built
         QtNetwork over wk smoltcp fabric, so that half of the original
         blocker has already been removed elsewhere.
      2. systeminformation_p.h:18 `#include <pwd.h>`, reached from
         kbugreport.cpp. wasi-libc has no account database, so getpwuid/getuid
         do not exist. It pre-fills the "From" field of the bug-report dialog;
         the WASI branch returns the same "Unknown User" placeholder the
         Windows branch already falls back to. Qt defines Q_OS_UNIX on this
         platform, which is what steers the header into its POSIX arm.
      3. kabstractaboutdialog_p.cpp:22 `#include <KSandbox>`. KCoreAddons does
         not build KSandbox on WASI (see kcoreaddons-0001) because ksandbox.h:7
         includes <QProcess>. KSandbox asks "am I inside a Flatpak or a Snap?";
         a wk node IS a sandbox, and a stricter one than either, but it is
         neither of those two -- so "no" is the correct answer and the
         packaging-format line is simply absent from the About dialog.
         Upstream has the same latent breakage on iOS, where KCoreAddons
         already excludes KSandbox for the same QProcess reason.
UPSTREAM: as-is for both. PrintSupport being optional is a plain portability
      improvement (Qt for embedded configurations routinely lack it), and the
      KSandbox include is an unguarded use of a KCoreAddons class that
      KCoreAddons own CMakeLists declines to build on two platforms.
diff --git a/CMakeLists.txt b/CMakeLists.txt
index 9a3827e..0f5c5d9 100644
--- a/CMakeLists.txt
+++ b/CMakeLists.txt
@@ -43,7 +43,19 @@ add_feature_info(DESIGNERPLUGIN ${BUILD_DESIGNERPLUGIN} "Build plugin for Qt Des
 
 # Dependencies
 set(REQUIRED_QT_VERSION 6.8.0)
-find_package(Qt6 ${REQUIRED_QT_VERSION} CONFIG REQUIRED Widgets Xml Network PrintSupport)
+# PrintSupport is OPTIONAL here, and it has to be: unlike Svg (a separate
+# repo, layerable into a port-local prefix) PrintSupport lives INSIDE qtbase,
+# so a Qt port that did not build it cannot gain it without rebuilding the
+# shared qtbase every other Qt node in wk depends on. wk's qtbase is
+# FEATURE_printsupport=OFF -- a wk node paints into one RGBA8 surface and has
+# no printer, no CUPS and no PDF spooler to reach.
+# Network IS present (plugins/qt built QtNetwork over wk's smoltcp fabric), so
+# it stays REQUIRED.
+# Exactly one file uses PrintSupport: kshortcutseditor.cpp's
+# printShortcuts(), the "print this shortcut list" button. See the guard there.
+find_package(Qt6 ${REQUIRED_QT_VERSION} CONFIG REQUIRED Widgets Xml Network)
+find_package(Qt6 ${REQUIRED_QT_VERSION} CONFIG QUIET OPTIONAL_COMPONENTS PrintSupport)
+set(HAVE_PRINTSUPPORT ${Qt6PrintSupport_FOUND})
 if(Qt6Core_VERSION VERSION_GREATER_EQUAL "6.9.0")
     find_package(Qt6 ${REQUIRED_QT_VERSION} CONFIG REQUIRED CorePrivate)
 endif()
diff --git a/KF6XmlGuiConfig.cmake.in b/KF6XmlGuiConfig.cmake.in
index 887c0c9..06c3f52 100644
--- a/KF6XmlGuiConfig.cmake.in
+++ b/KF6XmlGuiConfig.cmake.in
@@ -13,7 +13,15 @@ find_dependency(KF6GuiAddons "@KF_DEP_VERSION@")
 
 if (NOT @BUILD_SHARED_LIBS@) 
     find_dependency(Qt6Network "@REQUIRED_QT_VERSION@")
-    find_dependency(Qt6PrintSupport "@REQUIRED_QT_VERSION@")
+    # Only when this build actually had it -- see the PrintSupport note in
+    # CMakeLists.txt. A static KF6XmlGui records its private link dependencies
+    # here, and an unconditional find_dependency() makes every DOWNSTREAM
+    # find_package(KF6 COMPONENTS XmlGui) fail with "KF6XmlGui could not be
+    # found because dependency Qt6PrintSupport could not be found" -- naming
+    # the framework, not the missing Qt module.
+    if (@HAVE_PRINTSUPPORT@)
+        find_dependency(Qt6PrintSupport "@REQUIRED_QT_VERSION@")
+    endif()
 
     if (@KF6GlobalAccel_FOUND@)
         find_dependency(KF6GlobalAccel "@KF_DEP_VERSION@")
